// gui-core-07: an array whose element type is still open is claimed as a GUI.
//
// The gui package's is_custom (stdlib/graphix-package-gui/src/lib.rs:139)
// asks GUITYP.contains(env, &typ), a COMMITTING contains. The root type
// here is Array<'_N> with '_N unbound (not Array<_|_>), so the check binds
// '_N := &gui::Window and answers true (GRAPHIX_DBG_BIND=1 prints
// "BIND rhs '_N := ByRef(Ref(.. /gui/Window ..))" then
// "CONTAINS Array<&gui::Window> >= Array<'_N> -> Ok(true)").
// array::map([], |x| x) and `let f = |n| []; f(1)` are claimed the same way.
//
// command: env -u WAYLAND_DISPLAY -u DISPLAY \
//            graphix --no-cache design/review-2026-10-05/repro/gui-core-07.gx
//
// expected: `[]` printed, as `let a: Array<i64> = []; a` prints it.
// observed (HEAD c722befe, debug build):
//   Error: initializing custom display
//   Caused by:
//       0: creating the event loop
//       1: os error at .../winit-0.30.13/src/platform_impl/linux/mod.rs:765:
//          neither WAYLAND_DISPLAY nor WAYLAND_SOCKET nor DISPLAY is set.
//   exit 1. With a display the GUI starts with zero windows: the event loop
//   exits only on a window's CloseRequested or ToGui::Stop
//   (event_loop.rs:209-233), and the shell waits on the display's stop
//   (graphix-shell/src/input.rs:74), so nothing prints and nothing ends it
//   (Ctrl-C only interrupts the runtime).
//
// REPL (no display), the binding outlives the claim:
//   > let z = []
//   > z
//   error: initializing custom display ...
//   > let w: Array<i64> = z
//   type mismatch Array<i64> does not contain Array<'_N: &gui::Window>
// Without the `z` line in between, `w` is accepted and prints [].
[]
